Time Complexity - Rate at which time taken increases with respect to input size.
Time complexity is how fast an algorithm runs as the input size grows.
It does not measure time in seconds. Instead, it counts the number of operations or steps. 
This helps developers predict if code will slow down with large amounts of data.

Calculate tc always in terms of worst case scenarios, avoid lower values and constants.

Why Time Complexity Matters: 
Predicts Performance: Helps you know if code will crash on big data.
Compares Code: Shows which solution is most efficient.
Hardware Independent: Measures logic, not the speed of your computer.


Big-Oh notation - used for worst case time complexity
Theta notation - used for average time complexity
Omega notation - used for best case time complexity


1) O(n^2): Quadratic Time complexity means the number of steps increases exponentially as the input size grows. 
If your data size doubles, the program takes four times longer to run.

The easiest way to see is two standard loops where the inner loop runs completely for every step of the outer loop.

eg1: 

    // Outer loop runs 'n' times
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        
        // Inner loop also runs 'n' times
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            
            // This runs n * n times total
            // code
        }
    }


eg2: 


    // Outer loop runs 'n' times
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        
        // Inner loop runs 'i' times (grows larger with each outer turn)
        for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            
            // Basic operation
        }
    }

Let us trace how many times the inner loop runs if n = 5:
When i = 0: Inner loop runs from j = 0 to j < 0 → 0 times
When i = 1: Inner loop runs from j = 0 to j < 1 → 1 time
When i = 2: Inner loop runs from j = 0 to j < 2 → 2 times
When i = 3: Inner loop runs from j = 0 to j < 3 → 3 times
When i = 4: Inner loop runs from j = 0 to j < 4 → 4 times

If you add up all the steps, you get the sum of the first n-1 integers:
{Total Steps}=0+1+2+3+4+...+(n-1)
Formula = n(n-1)/2  = n^2-n/2
In Big O notation, we only care about the fastest-growing term and drop all constants:
Drop the division by 2: → n² - n
Drop the lower-order term (-n): → n²


Space Complexity : Space complexity is the total amount of memory space an algorithm uses relative to the input size.

Space complexity includes two main parts:
i) Fixed Space (Base Space): Memory that stays the same regardless of input size. This includes constants, simple variables,
and the code itself. Space you take to store inputs.

ii) Auxiliary Space: Extra or temporary memory allocated by the algorithm to solve the problem. 
Big O notation usually focuses on this space. Space you take to solve the problem
